Now all these tests are working as Expected, and all these tests are green; except in this last one:

ArrayListContractTest.cs(146,1): error CS0246: The type or namespace name 'PexObserve' could not be found (are you missing a using directive or an assembly reference?) [/root/test/bin/Debug/test.csproj]

I'm trying to fix this, but I don't know how.

A: If you are using .NET Framework as target framework and the PexForgivenessLevel level is set to Frustrating, then it means that Pex is not able to find all the necessary dependencies, or your project might have some problems that are breaking test discovery.

So for testing, you need to:


*

*Ensure you're targeting .NET 4.0 or later.

*Ensure your test project references System.Diagnostics.Contracts.

*Set PexForgivenessLevel to None in the PexSettings if you're targeting .NET 4.0 or later.

*You need to be using PexFriendlyAssemblies. The assemblies must be:
System.Core.dll 
System.dll 
System.Xml.dll 
System.Drawing.dll 
Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting.dll


*Ensure that the assemblies you are testing are referenced in your test project. 

*Make sure that the references path in test project is correct. Check that project references are correct -> properties -> build -> advanced -> specify other dependencies

*Update the Moles, or any other external code for all test methods are included and it should be in this namespace: [System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.PexMethodUnderTest]
If these still fail, try to run the tests with a different version of the ArrayList project you are testing.
Hope this helps.

A: I realize this thread is almost two years old but it seems no one has provided a solution from the provided solution. The first suggestion in the accepted answer helped get past some issues. However, adding the following two statements resolved the problem:
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Sdk;
and
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Sdk.Reflection;
These statements helped locate the missing dependencies in the form of PexObserve and PexAssert. Even though you would not use them in your code, they are necessary for Pex to generate the necessary tests.
One thing to note, Pex 2017 version no longer supports Visual Studio 2017, you can reference Microsoft's documentation on how to upgrade Pex to work in VS2017.
In your test project file (.csproj), you need to change all the <Import> elements that reference '*.targets' to reference '*_Pex.targets'. For example, you replace these:
<Import Project="..\packages\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.2017.2.1.0\build\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Pex.targets" Condition="Exists('..\packages\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.2017.2.1.0\build\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Pex.targets')" />

with:
<Import Project="..\packages\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.2017.2.1.0\build\Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Pex_Pex.targets" Condition="Exists('..\packages\Microsoft.VisualSt--------------------	    if (New_Ret == false && Old_arrListCount  !=  - 1) { Debug.Assert(1 == 0,"");}
	    
            AssumeFalse(() => New_ret);
        }
    }
}

